---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
const path = '/use-cases/vip-segments';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'use-cases/vip-segments');
const desc = description(site, 'use-cases/vip-segments');
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16 md:py-24">
    <a class="link" href="/use-cases">← All use cases</a>
    <p class="badge mt-6">VIP customer campaigns</p>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5 max-w-5xl">VIP messaging: qualify the member, honor the benefit</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9">A VIP program needs a defensible membership rule, a benefit the store can deliver, and a separate decision about whether a message may be sent. A high spend total alone settles none of those decisions.</p>
    <div class="prose-shopify mt-12">
      <h2>Define what the VIP promise buys</h2>
      <p>Write the customer promise first: priority access, a service appointment, a reward, or recognition. Specify who owns fulfillment, which products and markets qualify, whether membership guarantees anything, and how a member can see the terms. Distinguish a guaranteed allocation from an invitation to shop while stock lasts. Do not advertise reserved stock when a link merely opens a public product page.</p><p>The <a class="link" href="/guides/segment-by-value">value-segmentation guide</a> covers grouping customers by economics; the <a class="link" href="/use-cases/segmentation">segmentation playbook</a> covers audience mechanics. Here the job is to turn a qualified membership into a deliverable benefit without creating an unwanted message or an unfulfillable promise.</p>
      <h2>Qualify observed value and predicted value separately</h2>
      <p>For an observed-value rule, document the order window, evaluation time, eligible products, and treatment of tax, shipping, discounts, cancellations, refunds and returns. Use a named currency and conversion policy before comparing international orders. Net sales are not contribution: service-intensive purchases and expensive rewards can change the economics.</p><p>Resolve the customer identity behind the orders. A merged profile, shared telephone number or household account can attach someone else's purchases to a recipient. Hold ambiguous identities for review; never infer permission from merged spend. Keep the evidence and rule version that produced each membership decision.</p><p>A prediction needs its own label, prediction horizon, model version and uncertainty handling. Predicted future value is not money already spent or a promise of future purchases. Sparse histories and new customers should remain unknown when the evidence is insufficient. Consider a separately named newcomer benefit with its own budget rather than treating lack of history as proof of low value. Choose thresholds from your economics and promise; there is no universal VIP spend cutoff.</p>
      <h2>Keep membership, entitlement and permission independent</h2>
      <p>Store three separate states: membership under a dated rule; entitlement to a specific benefit; and permission for this channel and message purpose. A VIP may retain an earned benefit after withdrawing from SMS. Withdrawal should stop the affected messaging, not silently erase an earned entitlement. Explain any membership expiry under the program terms, and provide an appropriate way for members to check benefits without subscribing to promotional texts.</p><p>Before dispatch, check current channel permission, suppression, recipient timing restrictions, identity, and message purpose. Missing evidence means hold that message. Email permission does not establish SMS permission. Route policy questions through the <a class="link" href="/guides/consent">consent guide</a> and <a class="link" href="/guides/quiet-hours">recipient timing guide</a>; membership is no assurance of legal eligibility.</p>
      <h2>Make early access enforceable and capacity honest</h2>
      <p>Give each offer an identifier, access window with timezone, eligible variants, capacity owner, redemption limit and expiry. Enforce entitlement at redemption; a forwarded link should not grant someone else's benefit. If access uses a shared code, treat leakage as a risk to resolve before promising exclusivity. Decide how simultaneous checkout, reserved stock and failed payment release capacity.</p><p>For a limited service or stock pool, define allocation and waitlist rules before inviting members. An invitation is not a reservation unless the system actually reserves capacity. Pause invitations when inventory or booking data is stale. Tell customers what happens if capacity runs out; do not manufacture scarcity or imply a guaranteed reward to everyone in a larger audience.</p>
      <h2>Requalify membership and retire old queues</h2>
      <p>Record membership start, review or expiry time, rule version and the reason for qualification. Decide whether a refund changes future membership, an unredeemed reward, or neither under the published terms. Do not retroactively change an earned promise through an undocumented audience refresh. Give an operator ownership of disputed transitions.</p><p>Version queued campaigns against both membership rules and offer terms. A new rule must retire or explicitly revalidate the old queue; updating a segment label does not prove that an already exported job changed. Recheck eligibility and entitlement at dispatch and redemption. Use a deduplication key for member, offer and message step so retries cannot issue duplicate rewards.</p>
      <h2>Arbitrate competing messages and recover deliberately</h2>
      <p>A relevant purchase should cancel the pending invitation or reminder for that purchase. An open support dispute should pause promotional VIP nudges under your service policy. Permission withdrawal cancels affected queued messages. Keep those signals ahead of promotional priority, including when another sender owns the queue. A purchase need not end unrelated membership benefits.</p><p>When a VIP offer collides with a launch, loyalty reward or cart reminder, assign one owner and one winning purpose using current eligibility and combined contact pressure. A VIP label is not permission for an extra slot. Suppress the losing job and record why; see <a class="link" href="/guides/email-sms-coordination">channel coordination</a>.</p><p>After an outage, reconcile delivered messages, redeemed rewards, late purchases and withdrawals before resuming. Never replay an expired access invitation or automatically resend every failed job. Retire stale work, reconcile uncertain deliveries with the sender, and create a fresh eligible queue only if the benefit still exists. If entitlement enforcement fails, pause dispatch and redemption promises until capacity and access are reconciled.</p>
      <h2>Proposed acceptance fixtures: hypothetical, not merchant results</h2>
      <p>These scenarios are proposed checks for a program you design. Their conditions are illustrative, not recommended universal rules or evidence of app capabilities.</p><ol><li><strong>Refund and currency:</strong> a customer appears to qualify on gross orders in two currencies. Apply the documented conversion and refund policy; hold membership if either input is unresolved instead of comparing mixed totals.</li><li><strong>New customer:</strong> a first order lacks enough history for the value model. Mark prediction unknown; issue only a separately authorized newcomer benefit, if one exists.</li><li><strong>Withdrawal:</strong> a qualified member withdraws SMS permission after queuing. Cancel the text, preserve the earned entitlement under its terms, and do not silently switch to another channel without permission.</li><li><strong>Forwarded access:</strong> a nonmember opens an early link. Deny the restricted redemption without exposing member details. A valid member can still redeem within the stated capacity and window.</li><li><strong>Capacity:</strong> a hypothetical event has 40 bookable slots and 100 qualified members. Use the disclosed allocation or waitlist policy; never send 100 guaranteed-seat promises.</li><li><strong>Expiry:</strong> rule version B replaces A while A's invitation is queued. Retire A's job; assess a new invitation against B and current terms rather than relabeling stale work.</li><li><strong>Purchase and support:</strong> a member buys the offered item or opens a relevant service dispute before a reminder. Cancel the purchase reminder or pause promotion as appropriate, including in the other channel's queue.</li><li><strong>Recovery:</strong> dispatch times out after a reward was issued. Reconcile the reward and delivery record using the same deduplication key; do not issue another reward or replay an expired message.</li></ol>
      <h2>Measure incremental contribution after reward costs</h2>
      <p>Randomly assign eligible members to an invitation group and a comparable no-invitation holdout before dispatch. Keep assignment stable across channels for the agreed observation window and allow time for returns. Do not withhold benefits already promised to the holdout. If testing a new benefit itself, define a fair design before making promises. Distinguish the effect of reminding members from the effect of offering a different reward.</p><p>Hypothetical worksheet: 500 assigned members in each group produce $6,000 versus $5,500 in contribution before program costs, after consistent product cost, discounts and refunds. The difference is $500. If the invitation group adds $150 in rewards and fulfillment, $100 in messaging and allocated platform costs, and $50 in support, estimated incremental contribution is $500 − $150 − $100 − $50 = $200, or $0.40 per assigned invited member. Subtract only costs not already included; do not count a discount twice.</p><p>Those invented numbers demonstrate arithmetic, not expected lift. Report uncertainty, delivery failures, reward redemption, returns, opt-outs and support load with the contribution estimate. Retain assigned denominators rather than comparing only clickers or redeemers. A small VIP pool may not support a reliable conclusion; extend observation or report an inconclusive result rather than declaring a winner. Attributed VIP sales alone cannot show whether the invitation changed purchasing.</p>
      <h2>Choose tools around the benefit contract</h2>
      <p>An integrated stack can fit when one team owns membership, permissions, rewards and both channels, and can prove that updates reach queued work. Separate specialist senders can fit when an authoritative entitlement service and shared suppression process govern both. A service-led VIP program may fit a small operational queue when staff can reliably fulfill and reconcile each promise. Validate these conditions with your actual workflow rather than assuming a named app supplies them.</p><p>Use the <a class="link" href="/apps">app directory</a> to build a shortlist and the <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing">pricing worksheet</a> to price actual audience, reward and message volume. Ask each candidate to demonstrate the fixtures above, access enforcement, exports and recovery before purchase. Choose a stack that can honor your membership promise and retire stale invitations with clear ownership.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
